    sections: [
      { title: "What a Knowledge Panel Is and Why It Matters", content: "The Knowledge Panel is the prominent information box on the right side of Google search results. It includes your photo, biography, social media links, key facts, and related searches. For anyone researching you — journalists, producers, agents, brand partners — this IS their first impression. If it's wrong, missing, or incomplete, you're losing credibility before anyone reads a single search result. Our [complete Knowledge Panel guide](/blog/google-knowledge-panel-guide) covers everything you need to know." },
      { title: "How We Claim and Optimise Your Panel", content: "Step one is verification — we guide you through Google's claim process, establishing you as the authoritative entity owner. Once verified, we correct inaccuracies, update your biography, replace outdated photos, add official links, and ensure everything reflects your current career. We also apply entity SEO techniques — structured data, schema markup, and consistent entity signals — to ensure Google associates you with the right topics, roles, and accomplishments. This entity work directly improves how [AI search assistants](/services/ai-search-reputation) understand and describe you." },
      { title: "The Wikipedia-Knowledge Panel Connection", content: "Google draws most Knowledge Panel data from Wikipedia. If your [Wikipedia page](/services/wikipedia-for-celebrities) contains errors, those errors propagate to your Knowledge Panel — and from there into AI search answers. That's why Knowledge Panel management always includes a Wikipedia audit. Fixing one without the other leaves you vulnerable. When we manage both together, you get consistent, accurate information across every platform that matters." },
      { title: "Entity SEO: The Deeper Layer", content: "Beyond the visible Knowledge Panel, Google builds an entity graph — a behind-the-scenes understanding of who you are and how you relate to other entities. We optimise this through consistent schema markup, strategic content placement, and disambiguation techniques. The result: Google understands you accurately, which improves not just your Knowledge Panel but your entire search presence. For [actors and entertainers](/who-we-serve/actors-entertainers), [musicians](/who-we-serve/musicians-artists), and [athletes](/who-we-serve/athletes-sports-stars), this entity work ensures you're associated with your profession and achievements — not just the one controversy that happened to generate headlines." },
      { title: "What Lives Inside the Panel — and What You Can Actually Change", content: "A Knowledge Panel is assembled from dozens of data sources: Wikipedia, Wikidata, IMDb, Crunchbase, MusicBrainz, official social profiles, structured data on your own website, and Google's own entity graph. Once you're verified as the panel subject, you can submit feedback that directly influences the displayed photograph, the featured image carousel, the social profile links, the 'people also search for' suggestions, the description summary line, and key facts such as date of birth, education, spouse, and notable works. What you cannot change directly is the underlying source data — if Wikipedia says you graduated from the wrong university, the panel will keep pulling that error until Wikipedia is corrected. That's why every panel engagement begins with a full source audit, not a panel edit." },
      { title: "Common Panel Problems We Fix Every Week", content: "Wrong photograph, often a paparazzi shot or a decade-old headshot the client doesn't approve. Outdated job title, last role, or affiliation. Missing or incorrect 'spouse', 'partner', or 'children' rows that get prominent placement. Social handles linking to impersonator accounts or abandoned profiles. 'People also search for' carousel populated with controversies, ex-partners, or unrelated public figures. Featured news carousel dominated by a single negative story. Disambiguation collisions where your panel merges with a different person who shares your name. Each of these has a defined remediation path, and most resolve within one to three weeks of verified-owner submission. For ongoing protection, this work sits naturally alongside our [reputation protection retainer](/services/reputation-protection)." },
      { title: "Establishing a Panel When You Don't Have One Yet", content: "If Google has not yet generated a panel for you, it usually means your entity signals are too weak or too inconsistent for the algorithm to confidently identify you as a single notable entity. We build the missing signals: a Wikidata item with verified identifiers (IMDb ID, MusicBrainz ID, ISNI, VIAF, ORCID where relevant), a Wikipedia article where notability standards are met, schema.org Person markup on your owned website, consistent name and biographical data across every authoritative profile, and citations from at least 3–5 independent high-trust sources. Most clients see a panel generated within 60–120 days of this work. This is foundational entity work and feeds directly into your [AI search visibility](/services/ai-search-reputation) — the same signals that earn you a panel are the signals AI assistants rely on to describe you accurately." },
      { title: "Protecting the Panel After You've Won It", content: "A claimed and corrected panel is not a finished project — it's a living surface that updates whenever Google reprocesses your entity. A Wikipedia edit war, a viral negative story, a competing public figure with a similar name, or a poorly handled rebrand can all degrade or distort the panel within days. We monitor your panel daily, screenshot every change, alert on regressions, and re-submit corrections when needed. Combined with [Wikipedia change tracking](/services/wikipedia-for-celebrities) and AI search monitoring, this creates a closed loop where panel drift is caught and corrected before it propagates into Knowledge Panel mobile cards, voice-assistant answers, and AI summaries." },
    ],
